 along this yellow line  known as the line of actual control it's  not a hard border since china and india  have never actually agreed on where  it should lie but the ongoing  competition between them to build  infrastructure along it  continues to heighten tensions the two  militaries agreed in february  to pull back from the mountains around  the contested pangong lake  but china has so far refused to withdraw  from the other areas it has occupied  and india's ministry of external affairs  says the process of disengagement  remains unfinished  joining me now for more is defense  analyst and journalist  ajay shukla ajay what is the situation  on the ground  now well the situation  remains pretty much the same there has  not been a great  degree of disengagement and pullback by  either the chinese or the indians  there is one sector and that is it's  called the pangong lake sector  where a mutual disengagement and  pullback  has been agreed to but there are still  three more sectors  uh from the north counting down  uh the depsang plane uh the galwan  valley  and also further south towards dem choc  where the chinese have not pulled back  the indians have also  deployed forward now and to that extent  uh there's you can call it a token  disengagement  but the troops remain in contact troops  are facing up the dangers  of inadvertent escalation still continue  and to that extent it's not a very happy  situation on the line of control or the  line of actual control i should say  in ladakh uh does that mean that any  hopes that chinese forces will vacate  the areas that india wants them to  vacate are misplaced  well yes by by the looks of it they've  been in occupation of these areas  for more than a year now uh they have  not uh  been willing to discuss uh disengagement  from these areas  in the senior military officers border  talks that take place  periodically so by the looks of it uh  the chinese  seem to be saying that where we have  disengaged from where we want to  disengage  but the rest of the the areas where we  are in occupation  uh are chinese territory so it's it's  very much  a sort of a process of getting by  steady steadily creeping forward and  remaining in occupation  of those territories uh eventually they  just become chinese territories uh  after a few years i'm wondering then  just trying to expand this uh to the  region  what is the takeaway from this india  china standoff that has lasted more than  a year now  for countries in asia that also have  territorial disputes with china  well it's it's a very clear lesson isn't  it  just as they have done in the south  china sea  uh they have drawn a sort of an  arbitrary  unilateral nine dash line in 11-9  and they steadily come into possession  of islands and nettles by building  military infrastructure  in the areas that they claim they're  doing much the same thing in ladakh  they've come forward they've taken  occupation of the areas that they claim  and they're pretty much centered sending  out the signal that we're not  withdrawing from here this is chinese  territory  so there's there is a clear parallel  between the south china sea  uh except that that's in a maritime  context  and here on the land border in ladakh  honestly speaking ajay countries in the  region don't prepare to really have an  answer to this to these kinds of chinese  actions too there  oh no the only sort of difference that  you have here in this case  is that the indian army albeit belatedly  uh sort of went up occupied faced off  against the chinese  uh and sort of let the signal be known  that they will not allow any further  uh sort of a creeping possession by  china of indian claimed territory  so uh while in the south china sea china  is pretty much  getting a free run in the land border  the indian side has sort of put its foot  down and said that we are not letting  you get in possession of any more indian  territory  so uh it's it's uh it's a sort of a  nasty situation  but 